PURPOSE
───────
This file is read by installation programs to understand WHERE software
should be placed on this system. Three top-level hierarchies exist.
Choose correctly.


THE THREE HIERARCHIES
─────────────────────

  /usr    THE MACHINE ACTS ON BEHALF OF THE USER.
          ─────────────────────────────────────────
          Software here is what the machine considers necessary for the
          user's environment to function. The user does not curate /usr.
          The machine does. Package managers write here. System scripts
          write here. The user benefits passively.

          WHO WRITES:   apt, dpkg, system scripts
          WHO READS:    everyone
          PHILOSOPHY:   the machine serves the user silently


  /user   DIRECTLY FOR THE USER. BY THE USER. FOR THE FUTURE USER.
          ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
          Binaries, libraries, structs, organizations — all placed here
          by the user's own hand or explicit choice. This is where you
          build things for the future. For the future version of yourself.
          For the next project. For what comes next.

          This is NOT the machine acting on your behalf.
          This is YOU acting on your behalf.

          WHO WRITES:   the user (manual, JDesk, explicit install)
          WHO READS:    the user, user's programs
          PHILOSOPHY:   the user builds the future here


  /deck   PROFESSIONAL SYSTEM SOFTWARE.
          ─────────────────────────────
          Databases. IDEs. Artist programs. Professional audio. Security
          software. Scanning systems. Heuristics. Network-aware programs.
          Everything that operates at professional grade — system-aware,
          network-aware, security-conscious.

          WHO WRITES:   dedicated installers, JDesk, owner with elevated perms
          WHO READS:    system, network, Dave AI
          PHILOSOPHY:   professional tools that understand the system


DECISION RULES FOR INSTALLERS
─────────────────────────────

  ASK YOURSELF:

  1. Is this package managed by apt/dpkg with no user curation?
     → Install to /usr

  2. Is this something the user personally chose, built, or is
     building for their own future use?
     → Install to /user
     → MUST CONFIRM with the user before writing

  3. Is this professional-grade software? (database, IDE, creative
     tool, audio production, security, scanner, heuristic engine,
     network server/proxy/monitor)
     → Install to /deck
     → Register with Dave AI after installation
     → Check NEGAMANE brand before overwriting existing files


PERMISSION REQUIREMENTS
───────────────────────

  /usr    System-level permission (root / package manager)
  /user   Trusted or Genius permission class (owner-operator)
  /deck   Elevated permission (Trusted minimum, Genius for security tools)


NEGAMANE BRAND STATUS
─────────────────────

  /usr    BRANDED — immutable system entries, do not modify without gate
  /user   UNBRANDED — owner can modify freely
  /deck   BRANDED — critical professional tools protected


PATH PRIORITY
─────────────

  /usr/local/bin → /user/local/bin → /user/bin → /deck/local/bin → /deck/bin → /usr/bin → /bin


SURVIVAL ACROSS REINSTALL
─────────────────────────

  /usr    REPLACED on reinstall (machine rebuilds it)
  /user   PRESERVED (user's work persists)
  /deck   PRESERVED (professional tools persist)


EXAMPLES
────────

  gcc, ls, cp, libc.so           → /usr/bin, /usr/lib
  user's personal build script   → /user/bin
  user's custom data structs     → /user/lib
  MySQL server                   → /deck/bin
  ClamAV                         → /deck/bin
  Professional DAW               → /deck/bin
  JDesk IDE                      → /deck/bin
  Network monitor                → /deck/bin
  Vulnerability scanner          → /deck/bin


COMPANION FILES
───────────────

  ssetson.jpson    Machine-readable version of this guidance (JSON format)
  FILESYSTEM.md    Full documentation in markdown/FILESYSTEM.md


